WEBVTT

00:00:00.015 --> 00:00:02.701
Aesop’s Fables. Part 9.

00:00:02.701 --> 00:00:04.502
The Oak and the Reeds.

00:00:04.502 --> 00:00:11.430
An Oak that grew on the bank of a river was uprooted by a severe gale of wind, and thrown across the stream.

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:26.994
It fell among some Reeds growing by the water, and said to them, "How is it that you, who are so frail and slender, have managed to weather the storm, whereas I, with all my strength, have been torn up by the roots and hurled into the river?"

00:00:26.994 --> 00:00:39.660
"You were stubborn," came the reply, "and fought against the storm, which proved stronger than you: but we bow and yield to every breeze, and thus the gale passed harmlessly over our heads."

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:41.575
The Blind Man and the Cub.

00:00:41.575 --> 00:00:51.469
There was once a Blind Man who had so fine a sense of touch that, when any animal was put into his hands, he could tell what it was merely by the feel of it.

00:00:51.469 --> 00:00:57.063
One day the Cub of a Wolf was put into his hands, and he was asked what it was.

00:00:57.063 --> 00:01:08.977
He felt it for some time, and then said, "Indeed, I am not sure whether it is a Wolf's Cub or a Fox's: but this I know—it would never do to trust it in a sheepfold."

00:01:08.977 --> 00:01:11.506
Evil tendencies are early shown.

00:01:11.506 --> 00:01:13.418
The Boy and the Snails.

00:01:13.418 --> 00:01:24.387
A Farmer's Boy went looking for Snails, and, when he had picked up both his hands full, he set about making a fire at which to roast them; for he meant to eat them.

00:01:24.387 --> 00:01:34.576
When it got well alight and the Snails began to feel the heat, they gradually withdrew more and more into their shells with the hissing noise they always make when they do so.

00:01:34.576 --> 00:01:42.379
When the Boy heard it, he said, "You abandoned creatures, how can you find heart to whistle when your houses are burning?"

00:01:42.379 --> 00:01:44.720
The Apes and the Two Travellers.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:55.239
Two men were travelling together, one of whom never spoke the truth, whereas the other never told a lie: and they came in the course of their travels to the land of Apes.

00:01:55.239 --> 00:02:12.115
The King of the Apes, hearing of their arrival, ordered them to be brought before him; and by way of impressing them with his magnificence, he received them sitting on a throne, while the Apes, his subjects, were ranged in long rows on either side of him.

00:02:12.115 --> 00:02:17.387
When the Travellers came into his presence he asked them what they thought of him as a King.

00:02:17.387 --> 00:02:24.639
The lying Traveller said, "Sire, every one must see that you are a most noble and mighty monarch."

00:02:24.639 --> 00:02:28.727
"And what do you think of my subjects?" continued the King.

00:02:28.727 --> 00:02:33.981
"They," said the Traveller, "are in every way worthy of their royal master."

00:02:33.981 --> 00:02:39.270
The Ape was so delighted with his answer that he gave him a very handsome present.

00:02:39.270 --> 00:03:02.194
The other Traveller thought that if his companion was rewarded so splendidly for telling a lie, he himself would certainly receive a still greater reward for telling the truth; so, when the Ape turned to him and said, "And what, sir, is your opinion?" he replied, "I think you are a very fine Ape, and all your subjects are fine Apes too."

00:03:02.194 --> 00:03:08.974
The King of the Apes was so enraged at his reply that he ordered him to be taken away and clawed to death.

00:03:08.974 --> 00:03:10.990
The Ass and his Burdens.

00:03:11.605 --> 00:03:19.226
A Pedlar who owned an Ass one day bought a quantity of salt, and loaded up his beast with as much as he could bear.

00:03:19.226 --> 00:03:24.722
On the way home the Ass stumbled as he was crossing a stream and fell into the water.

00:03:24.722 --> 00:03:35.385
The salt got thoroughly wetted and much of it melted and drained away, so that, when he got on his legs again, the Ass found his load had become much less heavy.

00:03:35.385 --> 00:03:44.362
His master, however, drove him back to town and bought more salt, which he added to what remained in the panniers, and started out again.

00:03:44.362 --> 00:03:52.137
No sooner had they reached a stream than the Ass lay down in it, and rose, as before, with a much lighter load.

00:03:52.137 --> 00:04:01.270
But his master detected the trick, and turning back once more, bought a large number of sponges, and piled them on the back of the Ass.

00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:15.011
When they came to the stream the Ass again lay down: but this time, as the sponges soaked up large quantities of water, he found, when he got up on his legs, that he had a bigger burden to carry than ever.

00:04:15.011 --> 00:04:17.779
You may play a good card once too often.
